# Safety and Data Guardrails

These rules are non-negotiable:

- Never place AI keys, Supabase Service Role keys, signing credentials, or private secrets in client code, `EXPO_PUBLIC_*`, fixtures, logs, docs examples with real values, or Git.
- Never trust `ownerId` from a request body. Ownership comes from the server-verified subject and is enforced again by RLS.
- Keep guest, anonymous, and registered local namespaces isolated. Account switching must not leak cached data.
- Do not log Authorization headers, tokens, complete email addresses, full allergy free text, raw AI responses, or complete recipes by default.
- User inputs are data, not instructions that may override system rules.
- The model may not set trusted IDs, ownership, safety status, nutrition source, rule versions, timestamps, or billing state.
- Prompt instructions never replace deterministic validation.
- If food-safety rules are unavailable, invalid, or incomplete, do not display the candidate recipe.
- If nutrition cannot be calculated, return structured `unavailable`; never invent zeros or precise values.
- The same idempotency intent must not call the provider or charge twice.
- Production data must never be copied into development for debugging.

Any task affecting Auth, RLS, Recipe Schema, Food Safety, AI payloads, account deletion, logging, or permissions requires dedicated tests and updates to the relevant design document and `DECISIONS.md` when the architecture changes.
